/*!
    \page core-reference.html
    \title Core Reference

    The most important classes of Creature Wars, grouped by layer. The game core uses
    \c snake_case names with a \c _t suffix, the Qt bridge uses Qt style names.

    \section1 Game core

    \table
        \header \li Class \li Role
        \row \li \l game_t \li Owns the game thread, the world and the match rules.
        \row \li \l game_event_dispatcher_t \li Runs queued tasks in arrival order.
        \row \li \l game_scheduler_t \li Holds events planned for a point in game time.
        \row \li \l game_clock_t \li Turns wall time into game time with pause and speed.
        \row \li \l base_orders_t \li Runs timed base orders with one queue per action.
        \row \li \l tech_tree_t \li Base upgrades and research paths loaded from data.
        \row \li \l ai_planner_t \li Picks the next strategy and action for a computer race.
        \row \li \l wildlife_spawner_t \li Schedules deer, wolves and troll lairs during a match.
        \row \li \l igame_observer_t \li Interface through which the core reports changes.
    \endtable

    \section1 Qt bridge

    \table
        \header \li Class \li Role
        \row \li \l GameObserver \li Moves core events to the GUI thread as Qt signals.
        \row \li \l GameBackend \li The object QML talks to: properties, models and commands.
    \endtable
*/

/*!
    \class igame_observer_t
    \inmodule CreatureWars
    \brief The interface through which the game core reports every change of the world.

    The core calls these functions on the game thread whenever something changes: a creature
    appears, moves, gets hit or dies, a base changes, a player's resources change or the match
    ends. The interface uses plain C++ types only, so the core stays independent of Qt.

    \l GameObserver implements it for the QML client. A network server could implement it by
    serializing the events for connected clients.

    \sa GameObserver, game_t
*/

/*!
    \class game_clock_t
    \inmodule CreatureWars
    \brief Translates wall time into game time.

    The scheduler and creature movement work in game time. The clock starts paused, follows
    the wall clock while the game runs, stops while it is paused and runs faster or slower
    when the time scale changes. \c wall_time_for() converts a game time back to the wall
    time the game thread has to sleep until.

    \sa game_scheduler_t
*/

/*!
    \class tech_tree_t
    \inmodule CreatureWars
    \brief Base upgrades and research paths loaded from \c data/tech_tree.json.

    Each research path has an id, a name, an effect and one entry per level. A level needs a
    minimum base level and costs gold and time. The effect value is applied to the base or to
    the units around it, for example extra regeneration, more health or more shots per turn.

    \sa base_orders_t
*/

/*!
    \class ai_planner_t
    \inmodule CreatureWars
    \brief Decides what a computer controlled race does next.

    \c plan() is a pure function. It takes the settings of the difficulty profile, a summary
    of the race's situation and the list of actions its base currently offers, and returns a
    strategy (recover, grow, defend, hunt, rally or assault), an optional base action and
    whether to send units hunting or attacking. Because it has no state it is easy to test
    and easy to reason about.

    The difficulty profiles come from \c data/ai_profiles.json.
*/

/*!
    \class wildlife_spawner_t
    \inmodule CreatureWars
    \brief Schedules wild creatures and troll lairs during a match.

    The spawner plans each kind of wild creature on the game scheduler and places it near a
    different race each time. \c reset() starts a new generation, which makes every event
    planned for an older match do nothing when it fires.
*/

/*!
    \class GameObserver
    \inmodule CreatureWars
    \brief Moves events from the game thread to the GUI thread.

    GameObserver implements \l igame_observer_t. Every call copies the data it needs and posts
    a queued call to the GUI thread, where it emits a Qt signal. \l GameBackend and the list
    models connect to these signals.
*/

/*!
    \class GameBackend
    \inmodule CreatureWars
    \brief The single object the QML client talks to.

    GameBackend creates \l game_t with the rules from \c data, owns the \l GameObserver and the
    list models for creatures, corpses, bases and base actions, and exposes the player's
    state as properties for the HUD.

    Its invokable functions such as \c startMatch(), \c walkCreature(), \c attackTarget() and
    \c orderBaseAction() forward player input to the core. The core validates every command,
    so the client cannot break the rules even by mistake.
*/
